  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Soviet engineers lit a fire meant to burn three days. In 1971, a drilling rig collapsed into a massive gas cavern in Turkmenistan.",
        "visual_keywords": "desert terrain retro equipment, dusty landscape"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Fearing toxic fumes would poison nearby villages, scientists threw a match inside, expecting the gas to burn out by the weekend.",
        "visual_keywords": "spark igniting dark cave, flare burning"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Instead, the two-hundred-foot crater erupted into a permanent raging inferno. How do you extinguish a mistake that feeds itself?",
        "visual_keywords": "glowing crater rim, flames in desert"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Geologists severely underestimated the subterranean gas reservoir, which continually pumps high-pressure methane directly into the flames.",
        "visual_keywords": "smoke rising dark sky, fiery abyss"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Fifty years later, Turkmenistan's Darvaza crater is still burning. Since sealing the pit could trigger a massive explosion, will anyone ever risk putting it out?",
        "visual_keywords": "night sky over desert fire, glowing horizon"
      }
    ],
